- **Look-ahead:** I found none. All shifts are positive and entries all open at 00:00 after the signal day closes. `facts.json` reports no look-ahead bias and a stable recursive test on all three pairs.
- **Stored numbers:** trade counts, net profit and profit excluding the best trade all match `crosscheck.json` (BTC 33 trades, ETH 33, BNB 41). Exit reasons and durations look plausible. BNB''s `max_drawdown_pct__base` is 7.05 but its relative figure is 8.54. The gate used 8.54, and both are far under 25%.
- **Reliability:**
  - **BNB concentration:** one +381% trade supplies 84.7 of BNB''s 138.5 profit. Without it, BNB falls to 35.3, in line with BTC (39.9) and ETH (38.5).
  - **Hit rate:** 18 of BNB''s 22 losing trades are stop-losses, and BTC has 8 stop-losses. Profit depends on a few large trends.','2026-11-05T20:04:32.461342+00:00');

- **Gate verdicts:** The recorded decision is `candidate_for_portfolio` on BTC, ETH and BNB. Every gate passed, on the exploration split only. The note adds no recommendation.
- **Cost:** Going from optimistic to stress cost removes about 11–14% of net profit on each pair, and profit stays far above the 1.0 USDT bar. Cost does not explain the result.
- **Concentration:** The best five trades produce about 91% of net profit on ETH, 97% on BTC and 129% on BNB. BNB leans on one trade (+381%, about 61% of its profit). Across the best 10 trades, share of profit is 134%, 134% and 146% for BTC, ETH and BNB.
- **Win rate and exits:** Win rates are 16/33, 15/33 and 13/41. Stop-losses are 9, 11 and 21 of the trades, and the stop-loss exits are short.
- **Limits:** The samples are 33, 33 and 41 trades, barely above the 30-trade minimum. The three pairs trade overlapping dates, so they are not independent confirmations. Drawdowns reflect 0.2 exposure.

The shares and ratios come from my own arithmetic on the recorded numbers. I had no calculation tool, so I did it by hand. The method is stated next to each figure, and the sums are worth a quick spot-check.','2026-11-05T20:05:13.136763+00:00');

- **Drawdown:** every pair and cost scenario is far below the 25% ceiling. The worst figure is BNB stress at 9.29% relative drawdown. The losing stretches in the trade lists are the weaker point:
  - BTC had 5 consecutive losers over about 12 months, ending 2022-11.
  - BNB had two 5-loser streaks and a roughly 13-month stretch ending 2023-12.
  - BNB''s last 3 trades in the data are all stop-losses, about -12.2 USDT in total, with no recovery before the data ends.
- **Cost sensitivity:** net profit falls about 11–14% from optimistic to stress for BTC and ETH, and 10.6% for BNB. A linear extrapolation puts break-even costs far beyond the stress scenario. I did not read the scenario cost definitions, so I couldn''t turn that into a percentage cost.
- **Concentration:** removing the best trade cuts base profit by about 30% on BTC and ETH, but by about 75% on BNB. One BNB trade (+381%) and the one after it make up roughly 89% of BNB''s profit. The CSV amounts don''t match facts.json''s best-trade figures exactly, and I could not reconcile them from the files I read.
- **Overlap:** I compared H-025''s BTC trades with H-024 and H-009 by date only, with no correlation computed. H-024 was long in the same big trends, some with the same entry day, and one trade is identical. H-009 overlaps partially. I did not compare ETH or BNB lists, or H-019 and H-020.
- **Execution:** at roughly 5–6 trades per pair per year, a single missed or late order matters a lot. Stops fill at exactly the stop level with no gap slippage, which live trading may not deliver. Several re-entries happen within hours of a stop.

I created no other files and made no sizing or live-trading recommendation.','2026-11-05T20:06:10.897661+00:00');

