- **Gate verdict:** all three pairs (BTC, ETH, BNB) are `candidate_for_portfolio` on the exploration split in `facts.json`. Each has 80–95 trades, positive net profit in all four cost scenarios, and drawdown no higher than 16.4% (stress, BNB), against the 25% bar. Per `gates.json`, a candidate must clear the gates again on the validation split.
- **Cost:** going from optimistic to stress cost removes about 56–65% of profit on each pair. I derived that figure from the net profits in `facts.json`.
- **Concentration:** the best 10 trades account for more than 100% of net profit on every pair (BTC 127%, ETH 139%, BNB 224%). I summed `profit_abs` from the trade CSVs and divided by net profit. Because the stake compounds, these shares are only approximate.
- **BNB:** one trade, the 2021-02-17 entry at +92.4%, makes up 93% of BNB''s profit. Without it, profit falls from 39.38 to 5.36 at base cost and to −11.97 at stress cost. The negative stress value does not affect the verdict, because the gate only checks the base scenario.
- **Exit mix:** I counted exit reasons by hand from the CSVs. Stop-losses are 33% of BTC trades, 43% of ETH and 49% of BNB. Winners come almost entirely from the 48-bar time exit.
- **Correction to `validator.md`:** it guessed BNB''s best trade was the 10-day hold. That trade (2019-05-13, +39.7%) is actually the second largest.
- **Held-too-long exits:** `validator.md` flags time exits held far past 48 hours (2 on BTC, 0 on ETH, 6 on BNB by my count). I did not check the strategy file myself.
- **Not shown:** the report states that the results don''t show a stable per-trade edge. The pairs share many entry dates, so they are not independent samples. The 0.35 exposure derivation wasn''t in the files I read.','2026-11-06T12:59:32.740434+00:00');

- **Drawdown:** every pair and cost scenario is under the 25% ceiling. The tightest is BNB at stress, 16.41%. The worst losing runs are 7 straight BNB losses from 2020-11-02 to 2021-01-25 (about 12 weeks) and 6 straight BTC losses from 2018-07-29 to 2019-03-23 (about 8 months). The facts file doesn''t say whether the drawdowns are measured at the 0.35 exposure, so I didn''t assume it.
- **Cost sensitivity:** every pair stays profitable at stress, keeping about 41–49% of base profit. A straight-line extrapolation puts break-even near 1.4–1.5% round trip. That rests on two data points, so it is rough.
- **Concentration:** BNB depends on one trade, 2021-02-17, worth +36.80 of its 39.38 base profit. For BTC, the 2017-10 to 2017-12 trades are about half of base profit. The three pairs enter on many of the same days, so the 262 trades overstate the independent evidence.
- **Overlap:** I could only compare BTC lists, from H-025 and H-020, and the overlap is partial. H-040 BTC entries sit inside H-025''s long holds in several rallies, and a few fall inside H-020''s holds. I did not compute a correlation, and I did not read ETH or BNB lists from other hypotheses.
- **Execution realism:** stop-outs fill at almost exactly -3.34%, so the backtest models no gaps or slippage. About 12–14 trades per pair per year means a few missed fills or an outage would matter, and entries cluster across pairs.

Two figures don''t reconcile. My simple subtraction of the best trade differs from the facts file''s ex-best profit (BNB: about +2.6 against 5.36). I flagged this in the file as unexplained.

The file contains no sizing or go-live recommendation. I created no other files.','2026-11-06T13:00:27.311008+00:00');

- **`-1`:** a Williams-style volatility breakout on 1h. Buy when price closes above the day''s open plus 0.6 times the prior day''s range, in a 50-day uptrend, and exit at UTC midnight or on a stop.
- **`-2`:** an inside-bar breakout on 1h. Buy a breakout above a wide "mother bar" after a contained bar, and exit below the mother bar''s midpoint or after 24 bars.
- **`-3`:** a reclaim of the weekly anchored VWAP (volume-weighted average price) on 1h. Buy the first close back above the Monday-anchored VWAP after a sustained dip, in an uptrend.

All three sources are LuxAlgo Library pages, licensed free to use with attribution. Those pages give only the pattern or concept, so the lookbacks and thresholds in each file are my own fixed choices, not published values. The anchored-VWAP page gives no entry rule at all, so `-3` is my restatement and is the weakest of the three.

The turn-of-the-month idea was a candidate, but the one page I tried for it returned 404, so I left it out.

3 files written.','2026-11-06T13:29:40.327038+00:00');

